==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*skulaną||to owe}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*(s)kel-||shall, must, owe}}.  Cognate with Old High German {{term/t|goh|scolan}} (German {{term/t|de|sollen}}), Old Saxon {{term/t|osx|skulan}}, Dutch {{term/t|nl|zullen}}, Old Norse {{term/t|non|skulu}} (Swedish {{term/t|sv|skola}}), Gothic {{term/t|got|𐍃𐌺𐌿𐌻𐌰𐌽}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈʃulɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb}}

# {{context|transitive|lang=ang}} to [[owe]]
# {{context|auxiliary|lang=ang}} to be obliged, [[must]], [[should]]
#: ''Him æghwylc þara ymbsittendra ofer hronrade hyran scolde.''  All of the neighbouring tribes over the ocean had to listen to him.  (Beowulf ll.9-10)
# {{context|auxiliary|lang=ang}} [[should]], [[shall]], expressing the speaker’s intention
#: ''Þa cwæð he: Hwæt sceal ic singan?''  Then he said, ‘what shall I sing?’  (Bede)
# {{context|auxiliary|lang=ang}} [[shall]], to be going or about (to do something)

